I. INTRODUCTION
P
ARKINSON’s Disease (PD), the second most prevalent neurodegenerative disorder globally, primarily impairs motor functions [1]. PD is characterized by the gradual degeneration of dopamine-producing neurons, which results in motor symptoms, such as tremors, rigidity, bradykinesia, and postural instability. The rising prevalence of PD, largely due to the aging global population, poses significant challenges to healthcare systems worldwide. Current diagnostic practices primarily rely on clinical assessments, such as the MDS-unified PD rating scale (MDS-UPDRS) [2], which are inherently subjective and can result in diagnostic delays, especially during the early stages of the disease. The critical importance of early and accurate diagnosis in improving patient outcomes and quality of life has driven extensive research into new, daily monitoring methods for PD. Artificial intelligence (AI) [3] has emerged as a promising tool in medical diagnostics [4], particularly in detecting PD [5]. Machine learning facilitates the analysis of complex clinical data [6], enabling the creation of more accurate diagnostics [7]. AI systems can extract motor symptom patterns associated with PD from diverse data, such as speech, gait, and tremor analysis. Leveraging AI enables not only the detection of PD but also the monitoring of progression, paving the way for more personalized and adaptive treatment strategies. The Internet of Things (IoT) devices have made significant strides in reducing inference time (IT) and increasing processing power [8], with many systems achieving higher floating point operations per second for real-time data processing [9], [10]. Moreover, advancements in IoT have led to more energy-efficient devices, optimizing energy consumption without compromising performance [11], [12], which is essential for continuous, long-term monitoring. The widespread use of wearable technology [13], particularly smartwatches [14], has significantly advanced the potential for real-time monitoring of PD. Equipped with inertial measurement units, such as accelerometers and gyroscopes, smartwatches can capture detailed motor function data, including tremors, and gait. Monitoring in natural settings enables the collection of more accurate and consistent data than traditional clinical assessments [15]. Integrating these data with AI algorithms allows smartwatches to detect subtle signs of PD, classify motor symptoms, and potentially predict disease progression [16].
