Although smartwatches have demonstrated potential for PD detection [17], several limitations remain. A primary concern is the variability in data across individuals, influenced by factors such as user behavior. The inherent complexity of tremor data further complicates the problem, as tremor patterns often differ significantly between individuals with PD and healthy controls (HCs) [18], making classification difficult. Many existing methods struggle to address these variations, leading to reduced accuracy and reliability when using wearable data. Continued research is essential to refine these methods and improve their robustness for clinical applications [19]. This article proposes a method to distinguish individuals with PD and HC based on smartwatch data collected via accelerometers and gyroscopes. The method is built upon the multiscale frequency-sharing channel attention network (MSF-CANet), an end-to-end deep learning framework for PD detection. MSF-CANet includes a multiscale perception (MSP) module for extracting features at different temporal resolutions, enabling the detection of both fast and slow tremors. A frequency-aware (FA) module focuses on tremor signals in the 3–7 Hz range, while the shared channel attention (SCA) mechanism adjusts the importance of each sensor channel. To improve efficiency, a shared multilayer perceptron (MLP) is used within the attention mechanism. The final features are processed by a shallow convolutional neural network (CNN) for classification. The key contributions of this work include: 1) We propose MSF-CANet, which integrates the MSP and FA modules for effective temporal-frequency feature extraction, while reducing complexity through the SCA. 2) The impact of different smartwatch-recorded movements is systematically evaluated, identifying motion tasks, such as “drink from glass” and “cross and extend both arms,” as the most discriminative for PD detection. 3) The study investigates unilateral versus bilateral sensor inputs, demonstrating that left-hand data outperform right-hand data, and that combining both hands yields the highest classification performance. 4) Experimental results on the public dataset demonstrate that the proposed MSF-CANet significantly outperforms existing methods, achieving an accuracy of 92.39% and an area under the ROC curve (AUC) of 0.9797. The remainder of this article is organized as follows. Section II reviews related work on PD detection. Section III introduces the proposed MSF-CANet architecture. Section IV describes dataset details, evaluation metrics, and data augmentation. Section V presents and discusses the experimental results, including a comparison with existing methods and an analysis of the impact of different hands and tasks on detection performance. Finally, Section VI concludes this article.
II. RELATED WORKS
A. Inertial Measurement Units for Parkinson’s Disease Detection
Wearable devices have emerged as promising tools for the objective and continuous monitoring of PD symptoms, addressing the limitations inherent in subjective clinical assessments. Recent studies underscore their potential in capturing both motor
and nonmotor symptoms. For example, Kyritsis et al. [20] proposed a smartwatch-based method to measure Plate-toMouth movement elongation during eating, demonstrating its effectiveness in distinguishing PD patients from HC, with an AUC of up to 1.0 in real-world. Their approach utilized inertial sensors to analyze wrist micromovements, providing a noninvasive and scalable solution for PD assessment. Similarly, Oyama et al. [21] validated the use of a multisensor smartwatch for motor function assessment, showing strong correlations between digital measurements and clinician-rated scores. Their composite V-scores achieved an AUC of 0.748 for bradykinesia and 0.775 for tremor, highlighting the reliability of both clinical and home-based evaluations. Beyond motor symptoms, van Wamelen et al. [22] explored the relationship between wearable-derived bradykinesia and dyskinesia scores and nonmotor symptoms. Their findings revealed significant associations between gastrointestinal dysfunction and bradykinesia, suggesting that wearable sensors could provide valuable insights into the nonmotor manifestations of PD. Collectively, these studies demonstrate the versatility of wearable devices in advancing PD diagnosis and monitoring.
B. Voice for Parkinson’s Disease Detection
Voice-based detection of PD is emerging as a promising, noninvasive method for early diagnosis. Several studies have demonstrated that speech characteristics can be used to assess both motor and nonmotor symptoms in PD patients. Clark et al. [23] found that PD patients exhibit notable speech impairments, similar to those seen in progressive supranuclear palsy, highlighting the importance of speech analysis in PD diagnostics. Castelli et al. [24] utilized large language models to analyze spontaneous speech, achieving 98.00% accuracy in classifying “ON” and “OFF” medication states. Their study suggests that large language models, when combined with voice analysis, can offer valuable insights into neuropsychiatric fluctuations in PD. Subert et al. [25] examined the effects of dopaminergic therapy on speech, finding that treatment improved syntactic complexity and sentence length in PD patients. This research underscores the potential of automated linguistic analysis for tracking speech changes associated with PD and treatment responses. These studies demonstrate that voice analysis, particularly when combined with AI, holds significant potential for PD diagnosis and monitoring.
C. Machine Learning for Parkinson’s Disease Detection
Machine learning has proven to be an effective tool for the automated detection of PD. Omberg et al. [26] demonstrated the potential of smartphone monitoring, emphasizing the use of digital assessments to capture motor symptoms, such as tremors and gait disturbances. Their study showed that smartphone sensors, when combined with machine learning algorithms, could provide accurate predictions of PD severity, correlating well with traditional clinical assessments. Aggarwal et al. [27] proposed a two-phase hybrid machine learning model that utilized motor and nonmotor symptom data from the Movement Disorder Society Unified PD Rating
