III. METHODS
A. Overview
The MSF-CANet proposed in this article is shown in Fig. 1. The model is designed to classify PD and HC using accelerometer and gyroscope data collected from smartwatches. It integrates multiple modules to effectively capture and analyze tremor data. The MSP module extracts tremor characteristics over varying temporal scales, enabling the detection of both fast and slow tremors. The FA module amplifies tremor signals within the 3–7 Hz frequency range, a critical band for PD detection. The SCA mechanism dynamically adjusts the importance of each sensor channel, ensuring that the most informative channels are prioritized during classification. Additionally, a shared MLP reduces computational load. These components work in unison to address the challenges associated with tremor data from PD patients. The processed
features are then classified using a shallow CNN, forming an end-to-end framework that effectively distinguishes between PD patients and HC.
B. Multiscale Perception Module
