E. Fusion and Classification
The fusion and classification stage integrates the features extracted by the MSP, FA, and SCA modules, aiming to combine these multidomain features into a unified representation. The feature fusion process begins by concatenating the outputs of the three modules [35]. After fusion, the features are passed through a series of convolutional layers, each followed by batch normalization and pooling, to further refine the representations. These convolutional blocks progressively extract higher-level features while simultaneously reducing dimensionality. The output of these convolutional blocks is subsequently processed by global
average pooling, which reduces the feature map to a fixedsize vector. This pooled feature vector is then passed through fully connected layers, culminating in the final classification into PD or HC categories. By combining multidomain feature fusion with a shallow CNN, the model effectively learns to distinguish between PD and HC subjects.
IV. EXPERIMENTAL CONFIGURATION
A. Datasets
In this study, we utilized the PD Smartwatch (PADS) dataset [36], collected over three years during a crosssectional study at a large tertiary care hospital. The PADS dataset comprises data from participants diagnosed with PD, differential diagnoses [37], and HC, with extensive clinical annotations. Data were gathered using a multimodal smartphone application that integrated questionnaires and sensor recordings from smartwatches equipped with accelerometers and gyroscopes, sampled at 100 Hz. Participants performed a series of 11 motor tasks designed by movement disorder specialists to provoke subtle changes in movement pathology. Each task was recorded using two wrist-worn smartwatches and included motions, such as arm lifting and extension, finger tapping, and paced movements. The tasks are categorized into three motion types: 1) kinetic; 2) postural; and 3) resting. Following data quality guidelines, three tasks were removed due to data collection issues, resulting in a final selection of eight motor tasks, as listed in Table I. We focused on data from PD and HC participants. After excluding erroneous samples and performing age matching, the dataset comprised 469 cases, including 276 participants with PD and 79 HC. Data with differential diagnoses were excluded from the present analysis. Furthermore, tasks labeled “Relaxed,” “RelaxedTask,” and “Entrainment,” originally recorded for 20.48 s, were split into two segments for analysis. Fig. 3 shows the schematic of the eight selected maneuvers.
