C. Data Augmentation
To address the class imbalance in the PADS dataset, where the number of HC samples is significantly lower than that of the PD, targeted data augmentation techniques were employed. These techniques, including random scaling [38], random rotation, and noise addition, were specifically applied to HC data to enhance its diversity, thereby improving the model’s generalization ability and classification performance [39]. The choice of these augmentation techniques is directly linked to the nature of motor impairments in PD. In PD, tremors and motor function variability are influenced by factors, such as sensor sensitivity and the orientation of movement, which can vary among individuals. Thus, these augmentation techniques simulate real-world variations that may be observed in PD symptoms, enhancing the model’s robustness to these fluctuations and improving its ability to classify PD correctly. The random scaling modifies the magnitude of sensor readings by applying a scaling factor randomly selected within the
range of 0.8 to 1.2. This simulates variations in the intensity of movement, akin to the fluctuation in tremor severity often seen in PD patients, where tremors can vary in amplitude and strength depending on several factors, such as medication or activity level. Random rotation introduces variability in wrist orientation by rotating sensor data using randomly selected Euler angles along the “X,” “Y,” and “Z” axes. This augmentation reflects natural variations in wrist positioning across different subjects and is intended to model the variability in tremor direction and motor performance associated with PD, where motor control often exhibits irregularities in movement direction and coordination. To simulate sensor noise, Gaussian noise [40] is added to the data. The noise is scaled relative to the standard deviation of each channel’s data, ensuring the noise remains realistic without significantly distorting the original signals. This noise simulates the minor irregularities or fluctuations often encountered in real-world sensor readings due to tremors and involuntary movements. These augmentation techniques were selectively applied only to the HC samples to balance the dataset. The augmented data, along with the original samples, were stored in the same binary format to maintain consistency. By adding diverse variants to the dataset to reflect the manifestation of PD symptoms in the real world, the data augmentation process not only alleviates the class imbalance problem but also improves the robustness and overall performance of the model.
