V. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
A. PD Detection Performance
A nested 5-fold cross-validation strategy [42] was employed to evaluate the model. In this approach, the data is divided into five outer folds, and for each outer fold, there are five inner folds. Each inner fold contains 352 samples for training and 89 samples for validation, and is used to perform hyperparameter tuning. This means that for each outer fold, the data is further partitioned into five inner folds, where the model is trained and evaluated on different subsets to optimize hyperparameters. The key advantage of nested crossvalidation lies in its ability to avoid the “peeking-feature” problem [5], where information from the test set inadvertently influences the model during training. Such data leakage can lead to inflated performance metrics and poor generalization to unseen data. By separating the data into inner and outer loops, nested cross-validation ensures that hyperparameter tuning is performed independently of the final evaluation. The outer folds are used solely for final testing, after hyperparameter tuning is completed using the inner folds, providing a more reliable estimate. Fig. 4 shows the model’s performance across the five outer folds. Each subplot displays boxplots of key evaluation
metrics, where the central marker indicates the mean, the box shows the interquartile range, and the whiskers reflect overall variability. An aggregated summary of average performance across all folds is also provided, offering a comprehensive assessment of the model’s consistency and generalizability. The model demonstrates strong and consistent performance across the five outer folds of the nested cross-validation. It achieves an average accuracy of 92.39%, reflecting its overall ability to correctly differentiate between PD and HC participants. Precision averages 92.62%, indicating that the model produces few false positives when identifying PD cases. Similarly, the average recall is 92.40%, confirming the model’s effectiveness in detecting true PD cases while minimizing false negatives. The AUC reaches 0.98, indicating excellent class discrimination. A high AUC suggests that the model consistently ranks PD above HC across all decision thresholds. The boxplots of performance metrics for each fold exhibit limited variability, with most values tightly clustered around their respective means. This pattern suggests reliable model behavior across different data splits. Although minor outliers are observed—particularly in accuracy, precision, and recall— they likely result from natural variations in the PD dataset. AUC are especially stable, showing minimal dispersion and reinforcing the model’s robustness in class separation. The performance across all folds indicates that model is both effective and generalizable, with low standard deviations in the key metrics. This suggests that the model’s performance is not heavily influenced by particular subsets of the data and can reliably be applied to new, unseen data. The overall results are promising, indicating that the proposed model could serve as a powerful tool for real-time, wearable-based PD detection, offering a noninvasive and scalable solution. It is important to note that the PADS dataset does not currently provide severity grading for PD, thus limiting the ability to perform stratified detection of early and late stages within this dataset. However, early PD detection remains crucial for patient treatment and prognosis. Despite this limitation, our model demonstrates potential for early detection. MSFCANet, through the MSP and FA modules, is capable of effectively capturing tremor signals across different time scales and within the 3–7 Hz frequency range, which is critical for identifying subtle tremors in the early stages. Additionally, by continuously monitoring patients through wearable devices, the model can detect minor movement variations during daily activities, enabling the identification of tremor signals that are often missed during traditional clinical assessments.
