VI. CONCLUSION
In this study, we introduce MSF-CANet, a novel approach for PD detection using smartwatch sensor data. The proposed method achieves an accuracy of 92.39% and an AUC of 0.9797 by integrating multiscale temporal sensing, FA feature extraction, and adaptive channel attention. The results demonstrate that utilizing bimanual data significantly improves detection accuracy compared to one-handed use. Moreover, dynamic motor tasks, such as “drink from glass” and “cross and extend both arms,” prove more effective for PD detection than static movements. These findings underscore the strong potential of wearable AI systems for continuous, real-world monitoring of PD, offering an objective and scalable alternative to traditional clinical assessments. However, the dataset used in this study has some limitations, including a relatively narrow demographic and clinical variability, which may affect the model’s generalization across a broader population. Additionally, realworld deployment may face challenges related to sensor placement differences, which could influence the consistency and accuracy of the data. Furthermore, static tasks, while useful in controlled settings, may exhibit reduced sensitivity in detecting subtle tremor characteristics, thus potentially limiting the model’s performance in certain scenarios. Future research should aim to enhance the generalizability of the model across diverse populations and explore multimodal integration to further increase diagnostic accuracy. In addition to inertial signals, smartwatches can provide other relevant data types,
such as heart rate, heart rate variability, skin temperature, and electrodermal activity, which may offer complementary information about both motor and nonmotor symptoms of PD. Such advancements could lead to earlier detection of PD and more personalized treatment strategies, ultimately contributing to improved patient outcomes.
